&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;javascript:;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;标签管理&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;yui-content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;wiki-tab-0-0&quot;&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;http://scp-wiki.wdfiles.com/local--files/cone/image2.png&quot; alt=&quot;image2.png&quot; class=&quot;image&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;http://scp-wiki.wdfiles.com/local--files/cone/image3.png&quot; alt=&quot;image3.png&quot; class=&quot;image&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;http://scp-wiki.wdfiles.com/local--files/cone/image4.png&quot; alt=&quot;image4.png&quot; class=&quot;image&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;wiki-tab-0-1&quot; style=&quot;display:none&quot;&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;我投差评的常见原因&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;我的规则是：只要是你页面源代码里的东西，我就能根据它来投票。&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;一般来说我没有看到就差评的东西，但有很多东西会让我以负面的态度看待你写的文章。这些是描述我个人观点的指南——它们并不是硬道理，而且它们&lt;strong&gt;绝对&lt;/strong&gt;不&lt;em&gt;决定&lt;/em&gt;我的观点。I don&#039;t really have any instant-downvote triggers, but there&#039;s a bunch of things that&#039;ll make me see your article in a negative light. These are guidelines that describe my opinions - they are not hard rules, and they &lt;strong&gt;definitely&lt;/strong&gt; do not &lt;em&gt;define&lt;/em&gt; my opinions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;给大多数文章的指南&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;难看/偷懒的格式：&lt;/strong&gt;这个可能适用于网站上70%的文章，但如果你的文章只是一页文字的话，我就会先入为主地产生负面印象。如果你不能花时间让你的页面看起来在视觉上有趣一点，我又为什么要花时间去看呢？一张图是一个很好的打破单调的方式，可以让我在看之前就被吸引。 This probably applies to a good 70% of articles on the site, but if your article is just a page of text, I&#039;m already looking at it in a negative light. If you couldn&#039;t take the time to make your page visually interesting, why should I take the time to read it? An image is a great way to break the monotony and get me invested without even having to make me read anything.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;警告：Caveat:&lt;/strong&gt;你的格式需要和你的内容相匹配。一篇短的、简单的文章需要简单的格式。一篇长的、更详细的文章可以在“我就想这么弄”的格式上宽容一点。不要戴高礼帽穿T恤。 Your formatting should match the content. A short, simple article should have simple formatting. A longer, more elaborate article can get away with a bit more &#039;just because I want to&#039; formatting. Don&#039;t wear a top hat and a t-shirt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;排除别人：Excluding people:&lt;/strong&gt;这个网站的主要构成人员是美国人，所以如果一篇文章直接假设我就住在美国，而我其实住在英国，我就会感觉被排挤了。比如说，如果你说“SCP-XXXX位于北卡罗来纳”而没有点明“美国北卡罗来纳”，你在预设看这篇SCP文档的人在美国，引申开来整个基金会也就在美国，所以作为英国读者，我就没有被算在里面了。请确保你的地点是国际化的，我们就没事了。The majority of the site&#039;s userbase is from the US, so when I see an article that assumes that I&#039;m also in the US, as a UK resident, I immediately feel ostracised. For example, if you say &amp;quot;SCP-XXXX is located in North Carolina&amp;quot; without specifying &amp;quot;North Carolina, US&amp;quot;, you&#039;re expecting that the person accessing this SCP document is in the US, and by extension the Foundation is US-only, and therefore as a UK reader, I don&#039;t count. Make your locations international and we&#039;re good.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;请不要排除任何人，哪怕是无意的。Try not to exclude anyone, even unintentionally.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;缺乏视觉描述：Lack of visual description:&lt;/strong&gt;如果你在告诉我某个东西——它可能是一个物体、一个地点，什么都好——&lt;em&gt;求求你&lt;/em&gt;把它详细描述。视觉描述，对我来说，是看文的时候最重要的东西：我个人必须得在脑海里看见它才能恰当地理解它。如果你不描述它，我就会开始假设；而如果你打破了这些假设（一定会这样，因为你不知道我假设了什么），我就会感到疑惑。 if you&#039;re telling me about something - be it an object, an action, a location, or literally anything - &lt;em&gt;please please please&lt;/em&gt; describe it in detail. Visual description is the single most important thing to me when it comes to enjoying your article: I personally have to be able to see it in my mind to understand it properly. If you don&#039;t describe it, I&#039;ll assume things; and when you break those assumptions (which you will, because you don&#039;t know what they are), I get confused.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;很简单但也很容易遗漏——如果你的角色走进了一家咖啡店，我可能会假设收银台在右边，而当你说到你的角色对左边的收银员说话的时候我就会感到疑惑。描述房间的时候，请点出收银台在左边，我们就没事了。It&#039;s simple but easy to miss things - if your character walks into a coffee shop, I may assume that the cashier is on the right, and then get confused when you say your character talks to the cashier on the left. When you describe the room, specify that the cashier is on the left, and we&#039;re good.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;没有让我产生感情：Not making me feel something:&lt;/strong&gt;有很多写得很好的文章都很无聊。你可以讲述一个优雅、美丽的故事，但如果我没有感觉到什么情感或者我的观点没有改变的话，就很难得到好评了。如果我感觉我的时间被浪费在读一系列词语而不是令人信服的故事的话，我就很有可能会差评。 there&#039;s a lot of very well-written articles out there that are just dull. You can tell an elegant, beautiful story, but if I don&#039;t come away from that having felt something or having had my opinion changed, the chance of getting an upvote is very slim. If I feel like my time has been wasted reading a series of words rather than a compelling story, there&#039;s a big chance I&#039;ll downvote.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;文章末尾的评分模块：A rating module at the end of the article:&lt;/strong&gt;我坚信评分对于质量完全没有意义。所以，在文章末尾加上一个评分模块，就说明你不仅不同意我的看法，而且不相信我会记得评分。如果评分对你来说那么重要的话，你可能会需要这个。 I firmly believe that ratings are meaningless as a measure of quality. So, by sticking another rating module at the end of your article, you&#039;re telling me not only that you disagree, but also that you don&#039;t trust me to remember to vote. If votes are so important to you, maybe you&#039;ll enjoy another.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;让我失去兴趣：Losing my interest:&lt;/strong&gt;如果我发现我不想继续看一篇文章，我不是那种会想“好吧，如果我不读完整篇文章，我就没资格评分”的人。我会评分。 If I find myself not wanting to continue an article, I am not one of those people who thinks &amp;quot;well, I didn&#039;t read the whole thing, so I have no right to vote.&amp;quot; I will.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;给SCP的指南&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;用巨大的标题告诉我安保等级：Big headers telling me about a clearance level:&lt;/strong&gt;安保等级对你SCP的叙事几乎没有重要性，而如果你一上来就给我一张巨大的图片告诉我我需要4级权限什么的，我的心态会迅速从“开始体验一个有趣的SCP”转变成悲观主义模式。99.9%的所有SCP都有权限限制，那么为什么你的SCP需要特别标注权限？用你的大标题告诉我基本上&lt;em&gt;什么&lt;/em&gt;别的都好—— &lt;a href=&quot;http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/scp-3211&quot;&gt;我必须马上知道的事情&lt;/a&gt;，&lt;a href=&quot;http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/taboo&quot;&gt;会影响作为读者的我的重要收容措施&lt;/a&gt;，或者用它&lt;a href=&quot;http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/scp-3448&quot;&gt;给你的叙事提供语境&lt;/a&gt;。 Clearance levels are &lt;em&gt;very rarely&lt;/em&gt; important to your SCP&#039;s narrative, and when you open with a big image that tells me I need level 4 clearance or whatever, my mindset immediately switches from &amp;quot;open to experiencing an interesting SCP&amp;quot; to pessimist mode. 99.9% of all SCPs have a clearance restriction, why does your SCP&#039;s clearance level need special attention? Use your big header to tell me about &lt;em&gt;literally&lt;/em&gt; anything else - &lt;a href=&quot;http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/scp-3211&quot;&gt;something I need to know immediately&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/taboo&quot;&gt;important containment procedures that affect me as a reader&lt;/a&gt;, or use it to &lt;a href=&quot;http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/scp-3448&quot;&gt;give context to your narrative&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;作为一个常见物品的SCP：SCP is a common object:&lt;/strong&gt;如果我发现你的SCP是基于我家里能找到的一件东西，那你就有被差评的风险了。如果我感觉你的SCP&lt;em&gt;完全&lt;/em&gt;就是“我能把什么写成异常呢？”那么你的差评基本上就没跑了。同样，如果你告诉我这是一个异常物品但你不告诉我它的起源，那么也会给差评的天平上加上一块砝码。 If I could find the item that your SCP is based on in my house, you&#039;re on track for a downvote. If I feel &lt;em&gt;at all&lt;/em&gt; that the origin of your SCP is &amp;quot;what could I make anomalous?&amp;quot; then your downvote is pretty much guaranteed. Equally, if you&#039;re telling me about an anomalous object but you&#039;re not telling about its origin, then that&#039;s a big weight on the downvote scale.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;反过来说，如果你的SCP是什么不寻常的东西——比如说一个概念，一个地点，一个抽象的东西，那我就很有兴趣了。On the flipside, if your SCP is something unusual - like a concept, or a location, or something abstract, then I&#039;m all ears.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;“会”这个词：The word &amp;quot;will&amp;quot;:&lt;/strong&gt;毫无缘由的强制效果是偷懒的写法。SCP-XXXX会做这个，然后对象会做那个，然后X发生了，然后SCP会这样。你已经让我失去了兴趣。 Compulsion effects for the sake of them are lazy writing. SCP-XXXX will do this, the subject will then do this, then X happens, then the SCP will do this. You&#039;ve already lost my interest.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;测试测试测试：Testing testing testing:&lt;/strong&gt;基金会是收容的。我们不是光圈科技——一旦你做了足够收容某件东西的测试之后，就停手。 the Foundation contains. We&#039;re not Aperture Science - once you&#039;ve done enough tests to establish the most effective way to contain something, &lt;em&gt;stop&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;编辑：Redaction:&lt;/strong&gt;我在[这里]解释得更加清楚，但要义就是不要编辑掉任何东西，除非你已经清楚地解释了（或者即将在接下来的文章里清楚解释）为什么这里被编辑掉了。编辑人名和地名毫无意义（就随便编个名字不就好了！），而把编辑当成内容的一部分（“SCP-XXXX接着[已编辑]”）会得到即时的差评。 I explained my opinion better &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.scp-wiki.net/forum/t-7159089/your-unpopular-scp-opinions#post-3940816&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;, but the gist is don&#039;t redact anything unless you&#039;ve clearly explained (or will clearly explain, later in the article) why it&#039;s being redacted. Redacted names of people and places is meaningless (just pick a name/a random place!), and redaction as a substitute for content (&amp;quot;SCP-XXXX then proceeded to [redacted]&amp;quot;) can be an instant downvote.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;结尾的吓人笔记：Spooky notes at the end:&lt;/strong&gt;有太多的文章结尾是哦哦哦这笔记好吓人或者是乱码折叠或者之类的了。请缩成一团圆润地离开。给我想些更有创意的东西。 There are &lt;em&gt;so, so many&lt;/em&gt; articles that conclude with &lt;em&gt;oooo a spooky note&lt;/em&gt; or a corrupted-text collapsible or something. Please just fuck off. Come up with something more original.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Breaking every unspoken rule:&lt;/strong&gt; There are some articles that I instantly hate, but when it comes to justify that in writing, I can&#039;t do it. Sometimes I&#039;ll read an article, downvote it for eschewing some meta format without justifying itself, then think &amp;quot;well hang on&amp;#8230; why is that a bad thing? Why shouldn&#039;t that article do that? Who says you need to stick to this unspoken standard?&amp;quot; This happens frequently enough that I&#039;ve codified it as a guideline: an article that makes me go through that process &lt;strong&gt;does not&lt;/strong&gt; deserve to be instantly downvoted because of it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;给001提案的指南&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;无论如何我都可能会差评它：&lt;/strong&gt;001提案数目的膨胀，尤其是有些作者写了不止一篇，已经让这个曾经对我来说价值非凡的格式贬值了。因此，我对001的标准高得离谱，基本上什么都是差评。让我惊艳。任何一个我给出去的好评都是非常不稳定的——如果我读完了然后好评了，我可能会在一个月后回来，然后发现它其实没有那么惊艳，我就会取消好评。&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;给搞笑文章的指南&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;搞笑文章只有一条指南。我会无视其他所有的指南——我期待的也只是衍生和戏仿而已。只需要重视一件事：&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;不好笑：&lt;/strong&gt;如果我笑了，我就给好评，没有笑，就差评。就是这样。如果我还想说什么深刻的发言的话，我就会留下评论。&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;给中心页的指南&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;没有解释中心页的用途：&lt;/strong&gt;要是我读下来，你的中心页没有很好地让我知道我要写什么来给它做出贡献，你就会得到差评。要不然就是好评。&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;wiki-tab-0-2&quot; style=&quot;display:none&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;最近的主题&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;最近的版式&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;by &lt;span class=&quot;printuser avatarhover&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/esperanza-cai&quot;  &gt;&lt;img class=&quot;small&quot; src=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4113182&amp;amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;amp;timestamp=1607931041&quot; alt=&quot;Esperanza_Cai&quot; style=&quot;background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4113182)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/esperanza-cai&quot;  &gt;Esperanza_Cai&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
